1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,965
<font color="#FFFFFF">Equilibrium propagation is a promising energy-based
alternative to backpropagation for</font>

2
00:00:06,045 --> 00:00:09,833
<font color="#FFFFFF">training neural networks on brains or neuromorphic
hardware,</font>

3
00:00:09,913 --> 00:00:15,636
<font color="#FFFFFF">but it classically demands two things physical
substrates cannot easily deliver: perfectly</font>

4
00:00:15,716 --> 00:00:18,924
<font color="#FFFFFF">symmetric weights and infinitesimally small nudges.</font>

5
00:00:19,004 --> 00:00:24,533
<font color="#FFFFFF">This ICLR 2024 paper from the Friedrich Miescher
Institute and the University of Basel,</font>

6
00:00:24,613 --> 00:00:27,048
<font color="#FFFFFF">by Axel Laborieux and Friedemann Zenke,</font>

7
00:00:27,128 --> 00:00:32,850
<font color="#FFFFFF">cleanly separates the two resulting sources of
gradient bias and shows how to remove them.</font>

8
00:00:32,930 --> 00:00:36,912
<font color="#FFFFFF">Finite-nudge bias is eliminated exactly with a
Cauchy integral,</font>

9
00:00:36,992 --> 00:00:42,521
<font color="#FFFFFF">while weight-asymmetry bias is tamed by a new
homeostatic loss that penalizes asymmetry</font>

10
00:00:42,601 --> 00:00:45,100
<font color="#FFFFFF">of the Jacobian rather than the weights.</font>

11
00:00:45,180 --> 00:00:47,099
<font color="#FFFFFF">The payoff: for the first time,</font>

12
00:00:47,179 --> 00:00:52,837
<font color="#FFFFFF">equilibrium propagation trains asymmetric networks
on ImageNet 32 by 32 with only a small</font>

13
00:00:52,917 --> 00:00:54,900
<font color="#FFFFFF">gap to the ideal symmetric case.</font>

14
00:00:55,280 --> 00:00:59,602
<font color="#FFFFFF">Equilibrium propagation, or EP,</font>

15
00:00:59,682 --> 00:01:04,004
<font color="#FFFFFF">is an appealing way to train neural networks
directly on physical substrates like brains or analog chips,</font>

16
00:01:04,084 --> 00:01:08,407
<font color="#FFFFFF">because it computes gradients using only the
network's own relaxation dynamics.</font>

17
00:01:08,487 --> 00:01:12,809
<font color="#FFFFFF">But it comes with two strict requirements: the
weights must be perfectly symmetric,</font>

18
00:01:12,889 --> 00:01:17,211
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the nudge that pushes the network toward its
target must be infinitesimally small.</font>

19
00:01:17,291 --> 00:01:21,613
<font color="#FFFFFF">Both are very hard to satisfy in real physical hardware.</font>

20
00:01:21,693 --> 00:01:26,016
<font color="#FFFFFF">And crucially,</font>

21
00:01:26,096 --> 00:01:30,418
<font color="#FFFFFF">whether weight asymmetry actually harms learning
had never been pinned down,</font>

22
00:01:30,498 --> 00:01:34,820
<font color="#FFFFFF">because in practice its effect gets tangled up with
the error introduced by using a finite nudge.</font>

23
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:37,412
<font color="#FFFFFF">The reason this matters is energy.</font>

24
00:01:37,492 --> 00:01:42,334
<font color="#FFFFFF">Physical neural systems, whether biological brains
or neuromorphic chips,</font>

25
00:01:42,414 --> 00:01:47,660
<font color="#FFFFFF">could train networks at a tiny fraction of the
energy cost of digital hardware.</font>

26
00:01:47,740 --> 00:01:51,166
<font color="#FFFFFF">But backpropagation, the workhorse of deep learning,</font>

27
00:01:51,246 --> 00:01:56,896
<font color="#FFFFFF">needs a separate linear backward pass and the exact
transpose of every weight matrix,</font>

28
00:01:56,976 --> 00:02:00,604
<font color="#FFFFFF">neither of which physical substrates provide naturally.</font>

29
00:02:00,684 --> 00:02:05,593
<font color="#FFFFFF">EP sidesteps this, yet its own symmetry assumption
is nearly as demanding.</font>

30
00:02:05,673 --> 00:02:11,391
<font color="#FFFFFF">And there was a warning sign: the asymmetric
version of EP had only ever worked on toy</font>

31
00:02:11,471 --> 00:02:14,695
<font color="#FFFFFF">tasks like MNIST and outright failed on CIFAR-10.</font>

32
00:02:14,775 --> 00:02:16,380
<font color="#FFFFFF">Nobody had explained why.</font>

33
00:02:16,760 --> 00:02:20,514
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper makes four contributions.</font>

34
00:02:20,594 --> 00:02:24,347
<font color="#FFFFFF">First,</font>

35
00:02:24,427 --> 00:02:28,181
<font color="#FFFFFF">it analytically separates the two sources of bias
in generalized EP: the finite nudge</font>

36
00:02:28,261 --> 00:02:32,015
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the asymmetry of the network's Jacobian.</font>

37
00:02:32,095 --> 00:02:35,848
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second, it extends holomorphic EP to asymmetric,
complex-differentiable systems,</font>

38
00:02:35,928 --> 00:02:39,682
<font color="#FFFFFF">so the exact error can be recovered even without
weight symmetry.</font>

39
00:02:39,762 --> 00:02:43,515
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third, and most practically,</font>

40
00:02:43,595 --> 00:02:47,349
<font color="#FFFFFF">it introduces a new homeostatic loss that reduces
the asymmetry of the Jacobian directly,</font>

41
00:02:47,429 --> 00:02:51,183
<font color="#FFFFFF">rather than forcing the weights themselves to be
symmetric.</font>

42
00:02:51,263 --> 00:02:55,016
<font color="#FFFFFF">And fourth, it demonstrates that with this loss,</font>

43
00:02:55,096 --> 00:02:58,850
<font color="#FFFFFF">EP finally scales all the way up to ImageNet at
thirty-two by thirty-two resolution.</font>

44
00:02:59,230 --> 00:03:00,725
<font color="#FFFFFF">The method has two parts.</font>

45
00:03:00,805 --> 00:03:02,614
<font color="#FFFFFF">To kill the finite-nudge bias,</font>

46
00:03:02,694 --> 00:03:08,157
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors build on holomorphic EP: they drive the
network with an oscillating teaching</font>

47
00:03:08,237 --> 00:03:13,763
<font color="#FFFFFF">signal and use a Cauchy integral from complex
analysis to recover the exact error vector,</font>

48
00:03:13,843 --> 00:03:18,235
<font color="#FFFFFF">no matter how large the nudge and even when the
Jacobian is asymmetric.</font>

49
00:03:18,315 --> 00:03:23,148
<font color="#FFFFFF">Better still, this can be estimated continuously
over many oscillation cycles,</font>

50
00:03:23,228 --> 00:03:26,549
<font color="#FFFFFF">removing the need for separate free and nudged phases.</font>

51
00:03:26,629 --> 00:03:28,186
<font color="#FFFFFF">To tackle the second bias,</font>

52
00:03:28,266 --> 00:03:32,217
<font color="#FFFFFF">they show it grows with the skew-symmetric part of
the Jacobian,</font>

53
00:03:32,297 --> 00:03:36,375
<font color="#FFFFFF">and introduce a homeostatic loss that penalizes
exactly that part,</font>

54
00:03:36,455 --> 00:03:39,776
<font color="#FFFFFF">estimated efficiently with the Hutchinson trace trick.</font>

55
00:03:39,856 --> 00:03:45,193
<font color="#FFFFFF">The key insight is that this improves functional
symmetry of the Jacobian without ever</font>

56
00:03:45,273 --> 00:03:47,460
<font color="#FFFFFF">forcing the weights to be symmetric.</font>

57
00:03:47,840 --> 00:03:52,026
<font color="#FFFFFF">The experiments span four datasets of increasing
difficulty.</font>

58
00:03:52,106 --> 00:03:56,292
<font color="#FFFFFF">On Fashion MNIST,</font>

59
00:03:56,373 --> 00:04:00,559
<font color="#FFFFFF">small multilayer networks are used to cleanly
isolate and measure each source of bias.</font>

60
00:04:00,639 --> 00:04:04,825
<font color="#FFFFFF">Then a recurrent convolutional architecture with
genuinely asymmetric feedback weights is trained on CIFAR-10,</font>

61
00:04:04,905 --> 00:04:09,091
<font color="#FFFFFF">CIFAR-100, and finally ImageNet at thirty-two by
thirty-two,</font>

62
00:04:09,171 --> 00:04:13,358
<font color="#FFFFFF">which is where the homeostatic loss really earns its keep.</font>

63
00:04:13,438 --> 00:04:17,624
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors also confirm in the appendix that the
same loss helps predictive coding networks,</font>

64
00:04:17,704 --> 00:04:21,890
<font color="#FFFFFF">which have no reciprocal connections at all.</font>

65
00:04:22,270 --> 00:04:25,736
<font color="#FFFFFF">The headline result is striking.</font>

66
00:04:25,816 --> 00:04:29,282
<font color="#FFFFFF">On CIFAR-10,</font>

67
00:04:29,362 --> 00:04:32,828
<font color="#FFFFFF">the asymmetric network trained with EP but without
the homeostatic loss reaches only sixty</font>

68
00:04:32,908 --> 00:04:36,375
<font color="#FFFFFF">point four percent accuracy.</font>

69
00:04:36,455 --> 00:04:39,921
<font color="#FFFFFF">Add the homeostatic loss, and accuracy jumps to
eighty-four point three percent,</font>

70
00:04:40,001 --> 00:04:43,467
<font color="#FFFFFF">just four point three points shy of the fully
symmetric architecture,</font>

71
00:04:43,547 --> 00:04:47,013
<font color="#FFFFFF">and all with only approximate weight symmetry.</font>

72
00:04:47,093 --> 00:04:50,559
<font color="#FFFFFF">Even more importantly,</font>

73
00:04:50,639 --> 00:04:54,105
<font color="#FFFFFF">this is the first time this family of methods
trains at all on ImageNet thirty-two by thirty-two,</font>

74
00:04:54,185 --> 00:04:57,652
<font color="#FFFFFF">reaching thirty-one point four percent Top-1 and
fifty-five point two percent Top-5.</font>

75
00:04:57,732 --> 00:05:01,198
<font color="#FFFFFF">Throughout training,</font>

76
00:05:01,278 --> 00:05:04,744
<font color="#FFFFFF">the homeostatic loss steadily raises the Jacobian's
symmetry and tightens the alignment</font>

77
00:05:04,824 --> 00:05:08,290
<font color="#FFFFFF">between EP's error signals and true backprop.</font>

78
00:05:08,670 --> 00:05:12,558
<font color="#FFFFFF">The ablations cleanly separate the two biases.</font>

79
00:05:12,638 --> 00:05:16,527
<font color="#FFFFFF">On Fashion MNIST, when the nudge is made large,
classic one-sided EP falls apart,</font>

80
00:05:16,607 --> 00:05:20,495
<font color="#FFFFFF">its error ballooning to thirty-eight point four percent.</font>

81
00:05:20,575 --> 00:05:24,463
<font color="#FFFFFF">The holomorphic Cauchy-integral estimate with six
points instead stays at fourteen point three percent,</font>

82
00:05:24,543 --> 00:05:28,432
<font color="#FFFFFF">essentially matching the true derivative at
fourteen point seven percent,</font>

83
00:05:28,512 --> 00:05:32,400
<font color="#FFFFFF">proof that finite-nudge bias is fully removed.</font>

84
00:05:32,480 --> 00:05:36,368
<font color="#FFFFFF">Separately,</font>

85
00:05:36,448 --> 00:05:40,337
<font color="#FFFFFF">dropping from the exact derivative to a coarse
two-point estimate costs about three points on CIFAR-10,</font>

86
00:05:40,417 --> 00:05:44,305
<font color="#FFFFFF">quantifying the residual nudge bias.</font>

87
00:05:44,385 --> 00:05:48,273
<font color="#FFFFFF">And an architecture whose output feeds straight
back to the first layer,</font>

88
00:05:48,353 --> 00:05:52,242
<font color="#FFFFFF">with no reciprocal connections, benefits just as much,</font>

89
00:05:52,322 --> 00:05:56,210
<font color="#FFFFFF">confirming the loss targets functional symmetry
rather than weight symmetry.</font>

90
00:05:56,590 --> 00:05:59,441
<font color="#FFFFFF">To put the numbers in one place: on CIFAR-10,</font>

91
00:05:59,521 --> 00:06:06,411
<font color="#FFFFFF">the homeostatic loss lifts asymmetric EP from sixty
point four to eighty-four point three percent accuracy,</font>

92
00:06:06,491 --> 00:06:11,167
<font color="#FFFFFF">closing the gap to the symmetric network to just
four point three points.</font>

93
00:06:11,247 --> 00:06:13,577
<font color="#FFFFFF">On ImageNet thirty-two by thirty-two,</font>

94
00:06:13,657 --> 00:06:19,179
<font color="#FFFFFF">EP reaches thirty-one point four percent Top-1 and
fifty-five point two percent Top-5,</font>

95
00:06:19,259 --> 00:06:21,068
<font color="#FFFFFF">the first result of its kind.</font>

96
00:06:21,148 --> 00:06:23,804
<font color="#FFFFFF">And on Fashion MNIST, using a large nudge,</font>

97
00:06:23,884 --> 00:06:31,360
<font color="#FFFFFF">the exact holomorphic estimate cuts error from
thirty-eight point four percent down to fourteen point three percent.</font>

98
00:06:31,740 --> 00:06:37,623
<font color="#FFFFFF">The takeaway is that equilibrium propagation does
not actually need perfectly symmetric weights.</font>

99
00:06:37,703 --> 00:06:41,412
<font color="#FFFFFF">By removing finite-nudge bias exactly with a Cauchy
integral,</font>

100
00:06:41,492 --> 00:06:47,748
<font color="#FFFFFF">and by encouraging functional symmetry of the
Jacobian with a homeostatic loss instead of the weights,</font>

101
00:06:47,828 --> 00:06:54,084
<font color="#FFFFFF">asymmetric networks can finally scale to
ImageNet-level tasks with only a small gap to the ideal case.</font>

102
00:06:54,164 --> 00:06:56,569
<font color="#FFFFFF">Because functional symmetry is a weaker,</font>

103
00:06:56,649 --> 00:06:59,488
<font color="#FFFFFF">more achievable condition than weight symmetry,</font>

104
00:06:59,568 --> 00:07:04,768
<font color="#FFFFFF">this opens a biologically plausible path to
training real physical neural substrates,</font>

105
00:07:04,848 --> 00:07:08,930
<font color="#FFFFFF">and hints that brains might rely on similar
homeostatic mechanisms.</font>
